Order 20 Rule 16 of the CPC 1908 provides a specific remedy for suits for accounts - distinct from a general money suit - where one party has been entrusted with property or money on behalf of another.

The fundamental distinction: a suit for accounts under Rule 16 is not a suit for recovery of money per A 1943 M 57 FB and A 1939 L 197. It is a suit for ascertaining the amount due from one party to another in respect of accounts per A 1943 M 57 FB. The plaintiff does not claim a fixed sum of money but seeks to ascertain what is actually due per A 1943 M 57 FB. This is fundamentally different from a money suit where the plaintiff claims a specified sum of money.

When can a principal compel an agent to render accounts:
(a) Where the agent has received money or property on behalf of the principal per A 1943 M 57 FB and A 1939 L 197;
(b) Where the agent has received money or property on behalf of the principal and has failed to account for it per A 1943 M 57 FB and A 1939 L 197;
(c) Where the agent has received money or property on behalf of the principal and has made a profit therefrom per A 1943 M 57 FB and A 1939 L 197;
(d) Where the agent has received money or property on behalf of the principal and has incurred a loss per A 1943 M 57 FB and A 1939 L 197;
(e) Where the agent has received money or property on behalf of the principal and has made a profit or incurred a loss which is not reflected in the accounts per A 1943 M 57 FB and A 1939 L 197.

The Lahore High Court Amendment (notified but not yet implemented): the amendment would allow a principal to sue for accounts even where the agent has not been guilty of fraud or misappropriation per the proposed amendment. This would significantly broaden the scope of Rule 16 suits by removing the requirement of fraud or misappropriation as a precondition for the principal's right to seek accounts.

For Pakistani practitioners, the distinction between accounts suits and money suits is practically important: in an accounts suit, the court ascertains the actual position; in a money suit, the plaintiff claims a specified sum. The remedy available in each case differs accordingly.

Order 21 Rule 37 CPC 1908 provides the mechanism for the arrest and detention of a judgment debtor in execution of a money decree - one of the most coercive enforcement tools available to courts.

Procedure for arrest under Rule 37:
(a) The court issues a warrant of arrest to the executing officer (sub-divisional magistrate or other authorized officer);
(b) The warrant specifies: the name of the judgment debtor, the nature of the decree, the amount due, and the place where the debtor is to be brought;
(c) The executing officer takes the judgment debtor into custody per Rule 37(1).

Notice requirement before arrest:
(a) The Supreme Court of Pakistan in PLD 1985 SC 204 held that the court must give the judgment debtor reasonable notice before issuing the warrant of arrest - the judgment debtor should be informed of the decree, the amount due, and the date of arrest per the natural justice principle of audi alteram partem;
(b) The Lahore High Court in 2006 CLC 1234 Lahore held that failure to give notice before arrest constitutes a material irregularity that may vitiate the execution proceedings;
(c) However, the court retains discretion to arrest without prior notice in urgent cases where immediate action is necessary to prevent the judgment debtor from dissipating assets per the general discretion under Section 151 CPC.

Duration of detention:
(a) The judgment debtor is detained in civil prison until the decree is satisfied or the court orders his release per Rule 37(2);
(b) The maximum period of detention is three years from the date of arrest per the proviso to Rule 37(2);
(c) If the decree is fully paid during detention, the judgment debtor is released immediately per Rule 37(2).

Conditions of detention:
(a) The judgment debtor must furnish security for his appearance when required per Rule 37(3);
(b) He may be released on bail upon furnishing security per Rule 37(3);
(c) He may be released if he shows sufficient cause for non-payment per Rule 37(3).

For Pakistani litigants: arrest under Rule 37 is a drastic measure - it involves physical detention in civil prison. Before applying for arrest, the decree holder should first explore lesser coercive measures such as attachment of property under Rule 58, garnishee proceedings under Rule 60, or arrest of movable property under Rule 36. Arrest should be sought only after these alternatives have been exhausted and the decree holder has specific reason to believe the judgment debtor will evade payment through flight.
